    topic: "Screening a deal",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "An OM offers a 150-unit apartment building at $36M of guidance on a $1.98M T-12 NOI. What quick math tells you whether it's worth underwriting?",
    a: "Four quick checks: the going-in yield on guidance after resetting taxes to your price, price per unit against recent sales and replacement cost, that yield against the cost of debt and the NOI growth you'd need to hit your target. If the deal works at guidance only on heroic assumptions, pass.",
    why: "Screening is about spending underwriting time where you can win. A few ratios catch most mispriced deals. The adjusted yield shows what you earn on day one. Price per unit shows your basis against the cost of building new. The spread to the cost of debt shows whether leverage helps or hurts. For the return, unlevered IRR is roughly the going-in yield plus NOI growth when you exit at the same cap rate, so a 5% yield needs about 3% growth to reach 8%.",
    formula: "Adjusted yield = (T-12 NOI − tax increase) ÷ price\nUnlevered IRR ≈ going-in yield + NOI growth, with a flat cap rate",
    example: "Guidance is a 5.5% cap on the T-12. Taxes at [[1.1%]] of [[$36M]] are $396K against [[$250K]] today, so NOI falls $146K to $1.83M: a 5.1% yield. At $240K per unit, the price is 25% below a [[$320K]] replacement cost. Debt at [[6.25%]] costs more than the property yields, so leverage hurts until NOI grows.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what makes you pass fast? Guidance that needs rent growth well above the market's, a basis above replacement cost with new supply coming or a big lease expiry the price ignores."
