    classic: true,
    q: "Walk me through how you'd underwrite an acquisition, from the offering memorandum to a bid.",
    a: "Work from source documents, not the OM's pro forma. Build revenue from the rent roll: in-place rents, lease expirations, market rents, vacancy and other income. Rebuild expenses as the new owner would pay them, starting from the T-12. Add the capital plan: deferred maintenance, reserves and any value-add spending. Size the debt from lender quotes. Then run unlevered and levered returns and sensitivities, cross-check price per unit against replacement cost and set a walk-away price.",
    why: "The order follows what depends on what. Revenue comes first because rent drives value and most of the risk sits in lease terms. Expenses come next because the seller's costs won't be yours. Capital comes before returns because it's real cash the price has to absorb. Debt follows because loan size depends on the NOI you've built. Building up from the rent roll, rather than trimming the broker's NOI, keeps the seller's optimism out of your base case. The cross-checks catch what a model can hide: a strong IRR at a price far above replacement cost deserves suspicion.",
    trap: "Don't skip the tour. Walking units, roofs and the neighborhood catches what a data room can't: deferred maintenance, a weak location within the submarket or a competitor under construction down the street.",
