    topic: "Broker pro forma vs your underwriting",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "The broker's pro forma shows $2.40M of year-one NOI against a $2.00M T-12. How do you decide how much of that gap to pay for?",
    a: "Bridge the two line by line and sort each dollar by certainty. Pay fully for income that's in place or contracted. Put achievable upside, like below-market leases rolling to market, into your projection, where it arrives over time and costs money. Give little credit to upside that depends on the market or on savings you can't support.",
    why: "The broker's pro forma describes what the next owner could earn, and pricing on it hands the seller the value of your future work and risk. The bridge shows where the gap comes from, and each source earns different credit. A signed lease is nearly cash. Mark-to-market upside arrives only as leases roll and only if the market holds. Expense savings are credible only where a line runs above comparable buildings. Pay for the first, share some of the second and keep the rest as your return for doing the work.",
    example: "The gap: [[$120K]] of signed rent increases, [[$150K]] from rolling leases to market over two years, [[$80K]] from a utility bill-back program not yet started and [[$50K]] of payroll cuts. Credit the $120K and about a third of the rent upside in year one: $2.17M. At the broker's [[5.0%]] cap on $2.40M, the price is $48.0M, only a 4.5% yield on your year-one NOI.",
    trap: "Don't treat it as all or nothing. Buyers who pay nothing for upside lose every competitive deal; the skill is paying for the part you're confident you can deliver while keeping a return for delivering it.",
