    topic: "Bid rounds",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "How does a marketed sale run, from the call for offers to best-and-final?",
    a: "Usually in two rounds. After several weeks of marketing, the broker sets a call-for-offers date for non-binding first-round bids: price plus key terms like the deposit, diligence period, closing timing and financing. A shortlist then gets more access and submits best-and-final offers, sometimes with comments on the seller's draft PSA. The seller picks a winner.",
    why: "Two rounds let the seller build competition without wasting everyone's time. The first round is wide and cheap: bidders rely on the OM and data room, and the seller learns where the market is. The second narrows the field to serious buyers, who spend more on diligence and sharpen their numbers because there's usually no third round. The PSA comments show how hard each bidder will negotiate, and brokers call bidders between rounds to test who can close and to push prices up.",
    trap: "Best-and-final isn't always final. A seller can call another round or turn to the runner-up if the winner stumbles, so hold your walk-away price rather than raising it each time you're asked."
