    q: "How do you decide what to bid in the first round and in best-and-final?",
    a: "Set your walk-away price first: the most you can pay and still meet your return target on assumptions you'd defend at IC. In the first round, bid enough to make the shortlist while keeping some room below that. In best-and-final, bid at or near your walk-away if you want the deal, and compete on terms, not looser assumptions.",
    why: "Competition is how buyers overpay: each round tempts you to nudge rent growth or the exit cap until the model supports the number you need. Fixing the walk-away price first, from your base case, guards against that. The first round is about staying in cheaply, since bids are non-binding and the broker's feedback tells you roughly where you stand. Best-and-final is usually the last chance, so holding back risks losing to a buyer who paid only a little more. Then cross-check the price per unit against replacement cost and the yield against comps.",
