    topic: "Closing conditions",
    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "Your deposit has gone hard. What can still let you walk away and get it back?",
    a: "A failed closing condition or a seller default. Typical buyer conditions: the seller's reps are still true at closing, the seller has performed its obligations, the title company will issue your policy with only approved exceptions, estoppels meet the required threshold without material surprises, any lender consent is in hand and no major casualty or condemnation has occurred.",
    why: "Once diligence ends, the buyer has traded its free option for a contract, and the closing conditions are its remaining exits. Each protects against something that can change after the diligence window: a new lawsuit, a lien recorded against the property, a tenant disputing its lease, a fire. That's why buyers negotiate them hard. A financing contingency would be the biggest exit, which is why competitive sellers push buyers to drop it: without one, failing to get a loan is a buyer default, and the deposit is gone.",
    trap: "A failed condition gives you the right to walk and some leverage, but not an automatic price cut. And if the problem is the seller's default, you may prefer to force the sale rather than walk away."
